PART 1: ACADEMIC FORENSIC (Theoretical Answers)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Q1: Snippet A vs Snippet B (Initialization Lists)
- Snippet A creates the object with default values first, then modifies them
  (two steps: default construction + assignment).
- Snippet B uses the Member Initializer List directly, building the object 
  once and efficiently. (Snippet B is the correct and professional practice).

Q2: Shallow Copy vs Deep Copy & Rule of Three
- Without a custom copy constructor, the compiler performs a shallow copy,
  causing multiple objects to share the same heap pointer (leading to double-free crashes).
- A Deep Copy constructor allocates independent memory for each object.

Q3: Move Constructor vs Copy Constructor
- Copy Constructor (lvalue reference): Creates a duplicates of resources (higher performance cost).
- Move Constructor (rvalue reference): Transfers ownership of resources directly without reallocating (zero heap overhead).

Q4: Memory Leaks in Modern C++
- Occurs when dynamically allocated memory (`new`) is not explicitly freed (`delete`).
- Prevented in modern C++ using Smart Pointers (`std::unique_ptr`, `std::shared_ptr`) or RAII wrappers.
*/

